{"pair": "sigir-201515__NCT02428140", "eligibility": "eligible", "global_decision": 2, "rationale": "Inclusion criteria (per-criterion Stanford verdict):\n  - [met] Patient was hospitalized for cryptogenic stroke two weeks ago, which is an acute ischemic stroke. The diagnosis appears to have been made by a stroke specialist, given the work-up described.\n  - [met] The stroke was cryptogenic and confirmed by clinical work-up, but the note does not explicitly state that neuroimaging confirmed arterial ischemic stroke. However, CT was negative for hemorrhage and M\n  - [not met] No mention of transient ischemic attack (TIA) with diffusion weighted positive lesion on MRI; the event was a stroke, not TIA.\n  - [met] A 12-lead ECG was obtained and showed normal sinus rhythm. No mention of atrial fibrillation or flutter on any ECGs.\n  - [met] Patient is being actively investigated for the etiology of the stroke, as evidenced by echocardiogram and MRI/angiogram. Additional cardiac monitoring is desired to screen for occult paroxysmal atrial\n  - [met] Patient is 72 years old.\n  - [not met] No explicit mention of informed consent in the note.\n  - [met] No evidence in the note that the patient is expected to die within 6 months; she is stable and has resolved symptoms.\nExclusion criteria (per-criterion Stanford verdict):\n  - [met] No history of atrial fibrillation or flutter documented in the note, and ECG showed normal sinus rhythm.\n  - [met] No mention of planned carotid endarterectomy or stenting.\n  - [met] No mention of indication for long-term anticoagulation, pacemaker, or ICD device.\n  - [met] No mention that extended (>48 hour) external ECG monitoring has already been performed.\n  - [met] No evidence that stroke or comorbid illness will prevent completion of planned follow-up assessments; patient is stable.", "n_inc_assessed": 8, "n_exc_assessed": 5}
